# Aufwieder-zen — Cursor Rules

Aufwieder-zen is a Chrome Extension that modifies the LinkedIn feed. These rules
define strict technical boundaries for any code generated or edited in this
project. Follow them exactly; do not suggest alternatives unless the user
explicitly asks for a deviation.

## 1. Platform & Manifest

- This is a Chrome Extension built **strictly on Manifest V3**. Never generate
  Manifest V2 fields (`browser_action`, `background.scripts`/persistent
  background pages, `page_action`, blocking `webRequest` for network
  modification, etc.).
- The Service Worker (`background`) is the only allowed background execution
  context. Never assume a persistent background page.
- Keep `manifest.json` as the single source of truth for permissions, content
  script matches, and entry points. Any new capability requires an explicit
  permission added there — do not silently rely on undeclared permissions.
- Only request the minimum permissions needed (`storage`, host permissions for
  `*.linkedin.com`, etc.). Do not add broad host permissions like `<all_urls>`.

## 2. Language & UI Stack

- **Vanilla TypeScript only** for all logic. Do not introduce React, Vue,
  Svelte, Angular, JSX/TSX, lit-html, or any other UI framework or templating
  library.
- **Plain HTML and CSS only** for UI surfaces (popup, options page, injected
  UI). No CSS-in-JS, no styled-components, no Tailwind build pipeline, no
  template engines.
- DOM construction in TypeScript must use the native DOM APIs
  (`document.createElement`, `element.append`, etc.) or direct manipulation of
  static HTML — never a virtual DOM or framework runtime.
- Prefer small, explicit, strongly-typed functions over generic/dynamic
  patterns. Enable and respect `strict` mode in `tsconfig.json`.

## 3. Architecture: Strict Separation of Concerns

The codebase is organized into exactly four logical layers under `src/`. Code
must live in the layer matching its responsibility — never mix
responsibilities across layers.

- `src/popup/` — **UI and toggles only.** Renders the popup HTML/CSS and wires
  up user controls (toggles, buttons, settings forms). The popup reads/writes
  state via `chrome.storage.local` and may send messages to the background
  service worker. It must never directly query or mutate the LinkedIn page
  DOM.
- `src/content_scripts/` — **DOM manipulation and injection only.** Code that
  runs in the context of `linkedin.com` pages: reading the feed DOM, hiding or
  reordering posts, injecting UI elements into the page. Content scripts read
  configuration from `chrome.storage.local` (and/or messages from the
  background worker) but must not contain business/parsing logic that belongs
  in `utils`.
- `src/background/` — **Service Worker for cross-tab orchestration only.**
  Handles extension lifecycle events (`onInstalled`, `onStartup`), message
  routing between popup and content scripts, alarms, and any coordination that
  spans multiple tabs. It must not manipulate the DOM (it has none) and should
  delegate parsing/business logic to `utils`.
- `src/utils/` — **Pure parsing/business-logic functions only.** Functions
  here must be pure (no `chrome.*` API calls, no DOM access, no side effects)
  wherever possible: input in, output out. This is where feed-item parsing,
  filtering rules, and data transformations live so they can be shared and
  unit-tested independently of the browser environment.

When adding a new feature, first decide which layer owns each responsibility
and place code accordingly. If a function needs both DOM access and parsing
logic, split it: parsing goes in `utils`, DOM glue stays in
`content_scripts`.

## 4. State Management

- All persisted extension state (settings, toggles, filter rules, cached feed
  data) must be stored via `chrome.storage.local`. Do not use
  `chrome.storage.sync`, `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, `IndexedDB`, or
  cookies for extension state.
- Centralize `chrome.storage.local` access behind typed helper functions
  (e.g. in `src/utils/storage.ts`) rather than calling
  `chrome.storage.local.get/set` ad hoc from multiple files. Define a single
  typed shape for the stored state and reuse it everywhere.
- Use `chrome.storage.onChanged` to react to state updates across contexts
  (popup, content script, background) instead of polling.

## 5. Build & Tooling

- The project builds with **Vite** using the `@crxjs/vite-plugin` plugin,
  configured for a vanilla TypeScript Chrome extension (no framework
  plugins).
- TypeScript compiles with a strict `tsconfig.json` (`strict: true`,
  `noUnusedLocals`, `noUnusedParameters`, etc.). Do not weaken these settings
  to silence errors — fix the underlying type issue instead.
- Do not add bundler plugins for frameworks (React/Vue/Svelte plugins, JSX
  transforms) since none are used in this project.

## 6. Debug Logging Pattern

- **Always use debug wrappers for logging**, never call `console.log`,
  `console.warn`, or `console.error` directly. All console output must be
  controlled by the user-facing Debug Mode toggle in the popup.
- In most files, import and use `debugLog`, `debugWarn`, and `debugError` from
  `src/utils/debug.ts`. These are async functions that check the `debugMode`
  state from `chrome.storage.local` before emitting console output.
- **Exception: `blockAutomation.ts`** — Since this file is injected as a
  standalone function via `chrome.scripting.executeScript`, it cannot import
  modules. Instead, it declares inline `debugLog` and `debugWarn` helper
  functions at the top of `runBlockAutomation` that read `debugMode` from
  `chrome.storage.local` directly and conditionally call `console.*` methods.
- When adding or modifying logging statements:
  - Use `await debugLog(...)` for informational messages.
  - Use `await debugWarn(...)` for warnings.
  - Use `await debugError(...)` for errors.
  - In synchronous contexts where `await` is not allowed (e.g. event
    listeners, callbacks), use `void debugLog(...)` to fire-and-forget.
- Prefix all log messages with a consistent `LOG_PREFIX` constant (e.g.
  `[Aufwieder-zen:moduleName]`) to identify the source module in console
  output.

## 7. General Conventions

- Prefer named exports over default exports for utils and shared modules.
- Keep DOM selectors for LinkedIn's feed centralized (e.g. a constants file in
  `utils`) so markup changes only require updates in one place.
- Never add remote code loading (no `eval`, no fetching and executing remote
  scripts) — this violates Manifest V3 policy.
- Do not add comments that merely narrate obvious code; only comment on
  non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or LinkedIn-DOM-specific quirks.
